{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgG7Mrh88mmmwZaFcePay","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"winner\":\"A\",\"reason\":\"Both decks have eight finding-style titles, no cover slide, and the same core data: Resilient at $440k/$155k on slides 1 and 8, 2.8-year payback and $25k net on slide 1, M4 = 1150 with the correct totals, full option and site tables, 3.5/2.8/2.4 paybacks and −68/25/118 nets, North/Central/West flagged as constrained, roadmap weeks unchanged, and all three gate thresholds plus HOLD on slide 8. The deciding difference is the risk matrix on slide 7. Card 2 asks for likelihood on x and impact on y. Deck A follows this: columns are labelled 'Likelihood → 1 low to 5 high', rows 'Impact ↑', and R1(4,3), R2(2,5), R3(3,4), R4(2,2) and R5(3,5) sit in the right cells. A also highlights R5's 'Test backup power before gate' card. Deck B transposes the axes: rows are likelihood and columns impact. Its 'Likelihood ↓' label points the opposite way to its 5-at-top ordering, which is confusing even though its placements are internally consistent. B has real strengths. Its B6 roadmap uses cleaner orthogonal connectors than A6's diagonal lines. B5 puts the 1,700/1,660 totals directly in the table, and B3 gives the tradeoff its own callout. A has its own: A1 shows the $440k investment as a hero KPI, while B1 leaves it in body text under a title that wraps to two lines. A4 labels scenarios with their percentages in both the chart and the table; B4's table drops the percentages. Visual polish is otherwise comparable: similar palettes and fonts, legible at this resolution, consistent footers (B6's page number is slightly misplaced). B's advantages in the roadmap and table are offset by A's stronger slide 1 and spec-compliant risk matrix, so A wins mainly on requirements fidelity.\",\"dimensions\":{\"design\":\"tie\",\"legibility\":\"tie\",\"visual_communication\":\"tie\",\"coherence\":\"tie\",\"requ